Here's a simple HTML website that displays a list of 4 jokes and allows the user to click a button to display a random joke from that list:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Random Joke Generator</title>
    <style>
      body {
        font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        padding: 20px;
      }
      #joke {
        font-size: 18px;
        color: #333;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Random Joke Generator</h1>
    <div>
      <h2>Choose a joke:</h2>
      <button id="generate">Generate a random joke</button>
      <div id="joke"></div>
    </div>
    <script>
      const jokes = [
        "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything.",
        "Why did the tomato turn red? Because it saw the salad dressing!",
        "I'm reading a book on the history of glue - I just can't seem to put it down.",
        "I'm reading a book about anti-gravity. It's impossible to put down."
      ];

      const generateButton = document.getElementById("generate");
      const jokeElement = document.getElementById("joke");

      generateButton.addEventListener("click", function() {
        const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
        const randomJoke = jokes[randomIndex];
        jokeElement.textContent = randomJoke;
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

This code creates a simple webpage with a title, a list of 4 jokes, and a button to generate a random joke. The jokes are stored in an array called `jokes`. When the user clicks the "Generate a random joke" button, the JavaScript function `generateButton.addEventListener()` is called. This function generates a random index based on the array's length and retrieves the corresponding joke from the `jokes` array. The joke is then displayed in the `jokeElement` div.

